    "description": "A freight-forwarding platform models customer orders for multi-leg shipments. The OrderService is a Java (Spring Boot) module that records booking confirmations, container assignments and customs clearances. Business rules require that an order line can never be removed once a container has been assigned, that totals are recomputed whenever a leg changes, and that the lifecycle draft -> booked -> in-transit -> delivered -> closed rejects every illegal transition. Auditors replay 90 days of transitions from the event log. Today the order data lives in flat DTO maps shared by the pricing, planning and invoicing modules, so the rules leak into every caller and two modules disagree about what 'booked' means. The decision to implement: how to give one identifiable thing, with stable identity, an explicit lifecycle and enforced invariants, authority over its own state changes.",
    "decision_statement": "Give the order one identity-bearing owner that enforces its lifecycle transitions and invariants so callers cannot bypass the rules.",
    "notes": "Sibling set keeps near misses (aggregate vs entity) in play; the description names no pattern term."
  },
